# Vulnerability details

Note: In this submission, the vulnerability is referred to the solidity codebase. 

## Vulnerability details
Transaction ids between the handler contract and the settlement contract may not be unique, this may happens due to a lack of access control in `'ChakraSettlement::send_cross_chain_msg'` where users can send a cross chain transaction without passing from the handler. 

If users call `'ChakraSettlement::send_cross_chain_msg'` they won't be able to mint / unlock tokens on the destination chain since the field `'from_handler'` will result as the msg.sender, but malicious users can use this function in order to update for free their `'nonce_manager'` value in the ChakraSettlement contract. 

By doing so, when calculating the transactions ids, the `'nonce_manager'` of the user may be higher in the contract ChakraSettlement than the contract ChakraSettlementHandler, if this happens, transactions ids will be different.

## Impact
First of all, this vulnerability marks a data conflict between the handler contract and the settlement contract. 

But the major impact of this vulnerability regard the case where the source handler has a MintBurn mode. 

This is a problem because when the cross chain callback transaction is performed on the handler on the source chain, the field `'txId'` will be different from the `'txId'` registered in the handler contract. 

The impact is that the handler contract not having the settlement `'txId'` registered in the mapping `'create_cross_txs'` will return false and will not burn the tokens in the contract. If this happens some tokens will not be burned when they should be and they will be locked forever in the contract.

