## Proof of Concept
The u128_array_slice function in the codebase contains a critical bug in its length calculation. The function is designed to create a slice of a u128 array, but it incorrectly calculates the length of the slice.

```
fn u128_array_slice(src: @Array<u128>, mut begin: usize, end: usize) -> Array<u128> {
    let mut slice = ArrayTrait::new();
    let len = begin + end;
    loop {
        if begin >= len {
            break ();
        }
        if begin >= src.len() {
            break ();
        }

        slice.append(*src[begin]);
        begin += 1;
    };
    slice
}

```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-chakra/blob/d0d45ae1d26ca1b87034e67180fac07ce9642fd9/cairo/handler/src/utils.cairo#L427-L443

The bug lies in here:

```
fn u128_array_slice(src: @Array<u128>, mut begin: usize, end: usize) -> Array<u128> {
    let mut slice = ArrayTrait::new();
    let len = begin + end;  // This line contains the bug
    // ... rest of the function
}
```
Assuming end is meant to be the number of elements:

```
let source = array![1_u128, 2_u128, 3_u128, 4_u128, 5_u128];
let result = u128_array_slice(@source, 1, 3);
// Expected: [2, 3, 4]
// Actual: [2, 3, 4, 5] (or runtime error if out of bounds)
```

In this case, the function would try to create a slice from index 1 to index 4 (1 + 3), instead of taking 3 elements starting from index 1.

Assuming end is meant to be the end index:

```
let source = array![1_u128, 2_u128, 3_u128, 4_u128, 5_u128];
let result = u128_array_slice(@source, 1, 4);
// Expected: [2, 3, 4]
// Actual: [2, 3, 4, 5] (or runtime error if out of bounds)
```

Here, the function would try to create a slice from index 1 to index 5 (1 + 4), instead of from index 1 to index 4.

